<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Quadratic Functions to Describe Height</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01834</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Using Quadratic Functions to Describe Height</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2eccc9c8-abe8-42ed-b7c3-c4708dbeff0c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
        <para id="para-00051">1. The function defined by <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>d</m:mi>
              <m:mo>=</m:mo>
              <m:mn>50</m:mn>
              <m:mo>+</m:mo>
              <m:mn>312</m:mn>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mo>−</m:mo>
              <m:mn>16</m:mn>
              <m:msup>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mn>2</m:mn>
              </m:msup>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> gives
          the height in feet of a cannonball <m:math display="inline">
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:math> seconds after the ball leaves
          the cannon.</para>
      
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">a. What does the term 50 tell us about the cannonball?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00003">The 50 tells us the cannonball is fired from 50 feet off of the
            ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00004">b. What does the term <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>312</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> tell us about the
            cannonball?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00006">The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>312</m:mn>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> tells us that the initial
            upward velocity is 312 feet per second.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00007">c. What does <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            tell us about the cannonball?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00009">The <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                <m:msup>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                </m:msup>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            represents the effect of gravity pulling the cannonball back to the ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
     
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00010">2. Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the
            function. Adjust the graphing window to the following boundaries: <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
                <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>25</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> and <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
                <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                <m:mo>&lt;</m:mo>
                <m:mn>2000</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>.</para>
 <media alt="insert alt text">
        <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/>
      </media>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00013">
            <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis represents time in seconds and the y-axis represents the distance above the ground in feet.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_08_06_01.svg"/>
            </media>

          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

        <para id="para-00052">3. Observe the graph and:</para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00014">a. Describe the shape of the graph. What does it tell us about the
            movement of the cannonball?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00016">The distance from the ground increases, reaches a peak, and then
            decreases, which makes sense in the situation. It also looks like the distance from the
            ground decreases in the same way that it increased on the way up. The graph tells us the
            cannonball went up in the air, and then fell back down to the ground.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00017">The shape of the quadratic function is called a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">parabola</term>. It has a “U” shape.</para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00018">b. Estimate the maximum height the ball reaches. When does this happen?</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00020">The cannonball gets up to a little over 1500 feet, maybe to 1600
            feet, at around 10 seconds.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00021">The peak of the parabola, where the maximum occurs, is also called the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">vertex</term> of the graph.</para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00022">c. Estimate when the ball hits the ground.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00024">The cannonball hits the ground a little bit before 20 seconds.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <para id="para-00025">The <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00003">zero</term> of a
        function is where the function equals 0, or where it crosses the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-axis. In this case, there is only one
        zero where the cannonball hits the ground. The parabola does cross the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-axis in the negative <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-values, but that does not make sense
        in this problem.</para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00026">4. What domain is appropriate for this function? Be prepared to show
            your reasoning.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00028">The equation is a good model for the height of the cannonball from
            the time it is launched, <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>0</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>, until the time it lands,
            just before <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>20</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>. For times outside of
            these values, the function values do not mean anything in the context (for example, for <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                <m:mn>21</m:mn>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math> the function would place
            the cannonball underground).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <!--Interaction
    End -->
    <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
    will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <!--Start
        interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
        have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
        button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00029">If the cannonball were fired at 800 feet per second, would it
              reach a mile in height? Remember there are 5280 feet in a mile. Be prepared to show
              your reasoning.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00030">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00031">The equation here would be <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>50</m:mn>
                  <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>800</m:mn>
                  <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>16</m:mn>
                  <m:msup>
                    <m:mi>t</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msup>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.
              Since <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>g</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>10</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>6450</m:mn>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math> and there are
              5280 feet in a mile, this cannonball would reach over a mile in height. This could
              also be seen by graphing the function.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <!--End
    interaction-->
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Video: Using Quadratic Functions to Describe Height</title>
      <para id="para-00032">Watch the following video to learn more about quadratic functions.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://openstax.org/r/more-quadratic-functions"/>
      </media>

    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00011">
        <problem id="prob-00011">
          <para id="para-00033"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_7_6_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
