    <section id="sect-00006">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Reading Graphs of Quadratics</title>
      <para id="para-00035">An object is thrown upward from a height of 5 feet with a velocity of 60
        feet per second. Its height, <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>h</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, in feet after <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> seconds is modeled by the function <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>h</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>5</m:mn>
            <m:mo>+</m:mo>
            <m:mn>60</m:mn>
            <m:mi>t</m:mi>
            <m:mo>−</m:mo>
            <m:mn>16</m:mn>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>t</m:mi>
              <m:mn>2</m:mn>
            </m:msup>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00036">
        <media alt="A parabola on a coordinate grid. The x-axis represents time in seconds and the y-axis represents the distance above the ground in feet. The x-axis scale is 0.5 and extends from 0 to 4.5. The y-axis scale is 20 extends from 0 to 80.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_08_08_01.svg"/>
        </media>

      </para>
      <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> -Identify the starting height.</para><para id="para-00034">5 feet</para>
      <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> -Identify the maximum height by looking at
        the vertex or peak of the parabola.</para><para id="para-00048">approximately 62 feet</para>
      <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> -Identify when the object reaches its
        maximum by matching the maximum with its matching time from the <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>-axis.</para><para id="para-00049">approximately 1.75 seconds</para>
      <para id="para-00040"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> -When does the object hit the ground?</para><para id="para-00050">Look
        at where the object has a height of 0 feet, or the zero of the function. This happens at
        approximately 3.75 seconds. Notice there would be another zero on the graph of the function
        to the left of <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>x</m:mi>
            <m:mo>=</m:mo>
            <m:mn>0</m:mn>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>, but that does not make sense in the
        problem because the object was thrown from 5 feet.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Reading Graphs of Quadratics</title>
      <para id="para-00041">A rock is launched
        into the air, and its height is represented by the graph below.</para>
      <para id="para-00042">
          <media alt="A graph shows a parabola representing height in meters versus time in seconds. The x-axis scale is 0.5 and extends from 0 to 5. The y-axis scale is 5 extends from 0 to 30.">
            <image width="295" mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_08_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>

        </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00043">What is the maximum height of the rock?</para>
            <para id="para-00044"></para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00045">Here is how to find the maximum height:</para>
            <para id="para-00046">Look at the peak, or vertex, of the parabola.</para>
            <para id="para-00047">The maximum height is 25 feet after 2 seconds.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
  </content>
